Real, verified unified diff against the upstream file
browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs (checkout
target/gecko-src/gecko-dev, branch central-work @
5836a062726f715fda621338a17b51aff30d0a8c).

FIXES: "second and subsequent launches of an app come up with full Firefox
chrome" -- the longest-running open bug in the app-window work, reported
repeatedly from 2026-08 and finally root-caused on device 2026-09-25.

It was never the kiosk window features, and never `chromehidden`. Those are
applied correctly on every launch, as measured in the app window itself:

    at window load  chromehidden="menubar toolbar location directories
                                  status extrachrome"
                    toolbar.visible=false  nav-bar=0px  contentTop=1px

    ~2s later       chromehidden="extrachrome"
                    toolbar.visible=true   nav-bar=40px contentTop=41px

The window is born chromeless and then has its chrome flags rewritten out
from under it. The culprit is SessionStore restoring the previous session's
window features into the newly opened app window:

  ssi_restoreWindows
    -> _restoreWindowsInReversedZOrder
      -> _restoreWindowsFeaturesAndTabs
        -> restoreWindowFeatures(window, state.windows[0])

and restoreWindowFeatures() does, for every WINDOW_HIDEABLE_FEATURES entry,

    aWindow[aItem].visible = !hidden.includes(aItem);

With `aWinData.hidden` undefined -- which is what the app window's restore
state carries -- that is `.visible = true` for menubar, toolbar, locationbar,
personalbar and statusbar. Setting those from chrome rewrites the window's
chrome flags; AppWindow::ApplyChromeFlags() then regenerates the root
element's `chromehidden` attribute, which un-hides #nav-bar (40px of URL
bar, .chromeclass-location). It also flips `window.toolbar.visible` back to
true, which un-does TabBarVisibility.update()'s popup-like single-tab
treatment for good measure.

Why only the *second* launch: the restore needs something to restore. A
profile whose app has never run and quit has no session state, so launch 1
is clean; from then on every launch has a previous window to restore and is
chromed. (Once state exists, the first launch after a boot is chromed too --
verified. The reported "first launch is fine" is the first launch in a
profile's life, not per boot.)

The fix is upstream's own escape hatch. Firefox has this exact problem with
its own single-purpose app windows (Taskbar Tabs) and guards SessionStore
against it in four places, all keyed on one `taskbartab` root-element
attribute: the `isTaskbarTab` flag latched in onLoad() makes
initializeWindow() defer the initial state instead of restoring it, excludes
the window from the state collected in getCurrentState(), and keeps it out
of the last-session split at shutdown; restoreWindowFeatures() then
re-checks the attribute directly as a second line of defence.

A `pyxisapp` window is the same kind of thing and gets the same treatment.
This patch adds one module-level predicate, pyxisIsSinglePurposeAppWindow(),
and calls it at the two sites that read the attribute -- the flag latch in
onLoad() and the re-check in restoreWindowFeatures(). The stored field keeps
upstream's `isTaskbarTab` name so the other consumers are reached unchanged;
overloading it is deliberate and is what makes this a two-line behavioural
change rather than a four-site one.

Note this does NOT rely on the window being popup-like. initializeWindow()'s
first branch tests only `isPrivateWindow || isTaskbarTab` -- `toolbar.visible
== false` does not protect an app window there, which is why the kiosk
features alone were never enough.

Related, and deliberately NOT relied on: nova.rs's generated per-app user.js
already sets browser.sessionstore.resume_from_crash=false and
browser.startup.page=0. Neither prevents this path -- the restore observed
here ran with those prefs in force (opts={"firstWindow":true,
"overwriteTabs":false}). Prefs are not the fix; the attribute guard is.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
The unified diff below is generated from the checkout and is applied with
  git apply gecko/patches/pyxis/<path>.PYXIS_DIFF
(or `patch -p1 <`). Everything above this line is rationale, not content;
git apply ignores it. Regenerate with scripts/regen-gecko-patches.sh.
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

diff --git a/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs b/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs
index 517df0472760..7a34b66c96ef 100644
--- a/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs
+++ b/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs
@@ -109,6 +109,42 @@ const WINDOW_OPEN_FEATURES_MAP = {
   statusbar: "status",
 };
 
+// PYXIS: is this a single-purpose app window -- one that shows exactly one
+// app and must never become a session-restore target?
+//
+// Upstream already has this concept for its own app windows (Taskbar Tabs)
+// and guards SessionStore with it in four places, all reachable from the two
+// call sites of this function: the `isTaskbarTab` flag latched in onLoad()
+// gates initializeWindow()'s restore (it defers the state instead), excludes
+// the window from the state collected in getCurrentState(), and keeps it out
+// of the last-session split on shutdown; restoreWindowFeatures() re-checks
+// the attribute directly.
+//
+// A `pyxisapp` window (BrowserContentHandler.sys.mjs's openPyxisAppWindow(),
+// stamped onto the root element by browser-init.js) is the same kind of
+// thing, and needs those guards for the same reason. Without this, launching
+// an app a second time reproducibly came up with full browser chrome:
+// SessionStore restored the previous session's window features into the new
+// app window, and restoreWindowFeatures() sets
+//
+//     aWindow[aItem].visible = !hidden.includes(aItem)
+//
+// for every WINDOW_HIDEABLE_FEATURES entry. With `aWinData.hidden`
+// undefined that is `.visible = true` for menubar/toolbar/locationbar/
+// personalbar/statusbar, which rewrites the window's chrome flags, which
+// regenerates the root element's `chromehidden` attribute -- measured on
+// device 2026-09-25 going from
+// "menubar toolbar location directories status extrachrome" to just
+// "extrachrome" about two seconds after load, un-hiding #nav-bar (40px of
+// URL bar) and flipping `window.toolbar.visible` back to true, which in turn
+// un-does TabBarVisibility.update()'s popup-like single-tab treatment.
+// The kiosk window features were always applied correctly -- they were being
+// overwritten afterwards.
+function pyxisIsSinglePurposeAppWindow(aWindow) {
+  let root = aWindow.document.documentElement;
+  return root.hasAttribute("taskbartab") || root.hasAttribute("pyxisapp");
+}
+
 // These are tab events that we listen to.
 const TAB_EVENTS = [
   "TabOpen",
@@ -2003,7 +2039,10 @@ var SessionStoreInternal = {
       this._windows[aWindow.__SSi].isPopup = true;
     }
 
-    if (aWindow.document.documentElement.hasAttribute("taskbartab")) {
+    // PYXIS: `|| pyxisapp` -- see pyxisIsSinglePurposeAppWindow(). The stored
+    // field keeps upstream's name so every consumer of it below is reached
+    // unchanged.
+    if (pyxisIsSinglePurposeAppWindow(aWindow)) {
       this._windows[aWindow.__SSi].isTaskbarTab = true;
     }
 
@@ -6477,8 +6516,9 @@ var SessionStoreInternal = {
    */
   restoreWindowFeatures: function ssi_restoreWindowFeatures(aWindow, aWinData) {
     var hidden = aWinData.hidden ? aWinData.hidden.split(",") : [];
-    var isTaskbarTab =
-      aWindow.document.documentElement.hasAttribute("taskbartab");
+    // PYXIS: `|| pyxisapp` -- see pyxisIsSinglePurposeAppWindow(). This is the
+    // guard that was missing: the line below is what un-hid the chrome.
+    var isTaskbarTab = pyxisIsSinglePurposeAppWindow(aWindow);
     if (!isTaskbarTab) {
       WINDOW_HIDEABLE_FEATURES.forEach(function (aItem) {
         aWindow[aItem].visible = !hidden.includes(aItem);
